<header>
  <h1>Why Is the Sky Blue?<span class="zh">为什么天空是蓝色的？</span></h1>
  <p>For a toddler, a teen, and a physics PhD &nbsp;·&nbsp; 讲给小朋友、高中生和物理学博士听</p>
</header>

<div class="wrap">

  <!-- TODDLER -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="card">
      <span class="tag">3-year-old</span>
      <h2>For a 3-Year-Old</h2>
      <p>The Sun makes white light, and white light is actually all the colors mixed together — like rainbow sprinkles! When the sunlight flies through the sky, it bumps into tiny, tiny specks of air that you can't see. The blue colors bump around the most and bounce in all directions, so when you look up, all that bouncy blue light is coming right to your eyes. That's why the sky looks blue! At sunset, the light has to travel a longer way through the air, so the blue bounces off before it reaches you — and you get to see the reds and oranges instead.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="tag">3岁小朋友</span>
      <h2>写给3岁的小朋友</h2>
      <p>太阳发出的是白色的光，而白光是所有颜色混合在一起的——就像彩虹糖里的彩色糖粒！当阳光穿过天空时，会撞到空气里那些你看不见的小小颗粒。蓝色的光被撞得最多，朝四面八方弹来弹去，所以当你抬头看时，到处都是弹到你眼睛里的蓝光。这就是天空看起来是蓝色的原因！日落的时候，光要穿过更长的空气路程，蓝光还没到你那里就已经弹走了——所以你能看到红色和橙色。</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TEEN -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="card">
      <span class="tag">High school</span>
      <h2>For a High School Teen</h2>
      <p>The sky is blue because of <em>Rayleigh scattering</em>: as sunlight passes through the atmosphere, molecules in the air (mainly N₂ and O₂) scatter light. The amount of scattering depends strongly on wavelength — roughly proportional to <em>1/λ⁴</em> — meaning shorter wavelengths are scattered far more than longer ones. Blue light (~450 nm) is scattered about 10 times more than red light (~650 nm). So blue light gets redirected in all directions across the sky, and that's what reaches your eyes no matter where you look.</p>
      <p><strong>Common follow-ups:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Why isn't the sky violet?</strong> Violet light is scattered even more than blue, but the Sun emits much less violet than blue, and your eyes are less sensitive to violet. Blue wins the competition.</li>
        <li><strong>Why is the Sun yellow, and sunsets red?</strong> Direct sunlight has had its blue component scattered away, giving a yellowish tint. At sunset, light travels a much longer path through the atmosphere, so nearly all the blue is scattered out before it reaches you, leaving reds and oranges.</li>
        <li><strong>Why are clouds white?</strong> Cloud droplets are much larger than the wavelength of light, so they scatter <em>all</em> wavelengths equally (<em>Mie scattering</em>), reflecting white light back as a whole.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="tag">高中生</span>
      <h2>写给高中生</h2>
      <p>天空是蓝色的，是因为<em>瑞利散射</em>：当阳光穿过大气层时，空气中的分子（主要是 N₂ 和 O₂）会散射光线。散射程度强烈依赖于波长——大致与 <em>1/λ⁴</em> 成正比——也就是说，短波长的光比长波长的光被散射得多得多。蓝光（约 450 纳米）被散射的量大约是红光（约 650 纳米）的 10 倍。因此蓝光被朝天空各个方向偏转，无论你朝哪里看，进入眼睛的都是这些光。</p>
      <p><strong>几个常见的追问：</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>天空为什么不是紫色的？</strong>紫光比蓝光被散射得还要多，但太阳发出的紫光比蓝光少得多（太阳光谱在黄绿光处达到峰值），而且人眼对紫光的敏感度也较低。蓝色在竞争中胜出。</li>
        <li><strong>太阳为什么是黄色的，日落时又为什么是红色的？</strong>直射阳光中的蓝光成分已被散射掉，因此呈现黄色调。日落时，光要穿过厚得多的大气层，蓝光在到达你之前几乎全部被散射掉，只剩下红色和橙色。</li>
        <li><strong>云为什么是白色的？</strong>云滴的尺寸比光的波长大得多，因此它们对<em>所有</em>波长的光散射程度基本相同（<em>米氏散射</em>），把白光整体反射回来。</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PHD -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="card">
      <span class="tag">Physics PhD</span>
      <h2>For a Physics PhD</h2>
      <p>The phenomenon is classical <em>Rayleigh scattering</em> from molecules whose size <i>a</i> is much smaller than the wavelength <i>λ</i> (a/λ ≪ 1). The incident electric field induces a polarization with polarizability α, and the re-radiated field is that of a Hertzian dipole. The differential scattering cross-section is:</p>
      <div class="formula">
        <span class="fr">dσ/dΩ = (k⁴ / 4π²ε₀²)² · |α|² / r² · (1 + cos²θ) / 2</span>
        <small>with k = 2π/λ, giving the characteristic λ⁻⁴ wavelength dependence.</small>
      </div>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Cross-section scaling.</strong> Since σ ∝ λ⁻⁴, at 400 nm vs 700 nm the scattering ratio is (700/400)⁴ ≈ 8×. Combined with the solar spectral radiance and the photopic sensitivity curve <i>V</i>(λ), blue dominates the observed scattered skylight.</li>
        <li><strong>Anisotropy &amp; depolarization.</strong> Air molecules (N₂, O₂) have anisotropic polarizability tensors, so linearly polarized light is partially depolarized on scattering; the degree of depolarization is ~1.4% for air. The sky's polarization (maximal 90° from the Sun) is a direct consequence of the dipole radiation pattern.</li>
        <li><strong>Optical depth &amp; angular dependence.</strong> Observed radiance depends on the optical depth τ(λ) along the line of sight; longer slant paths at low solar elevation (sec θ₀, with exact treatment for spherical atmospheres) explain the reddening of the Sun and enhanced red/orange hues when τ(λ_blue) ≫ 1.</li>
        <li><strong>Regime boundaries.</strong> The Rayleigh approximation breaks down when 2πa/λ ~ 1; for cloud droplets (a ~ 1–100 μm ≫ λ), <em>Mie scattering</em> applies and is nearly wavelength-independent, yielding white clouds. Rayleigh scattering also explains the blue of the eye's vitreous humor and gas-giant skies (methane absorption modifies the picture on Neptune/Uranus).</li>
        <li><strong>Molecular form factor.</strong> Strictly, α is frequency-dependent (Lorentz-oscillator dispersion), but across the visible for N₂ and O₂ the variation is weak compared to the explicit k⁴ factor, so λ⁻⁴ holds to within a few percent. Quantum mechanically, the same result follows from second-order perturbation theory for the polarizability; Raman lines (Bjerknes/Raman) appear as a small structured component outside the elastic Rayleigh background.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>In short:</strong> the sky is blue because elastic scattering from small molecules carries a λ⁻⁴ weighting that, convolved with the solar spectrum and the eye's spectral response, peaks perceptually in the blue.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <span class="tag">物理学博士</span>
      <h2>写给物理学博士</h2>
      <p>这一现象是分子尺寸 <i>a</i> 远小于入射光波长 <i>λ</i>（a/λ ≪ 1）时的经典<em>瑞利散射</em>。入射电场在分子上感应出极化，极化率为 α，再辐射场即为赫兹偶极子的辐射场。微分散射截面为：</p>
      <div class="formula">
        <span class="fr">dσ/dΩ = (k⁴ / 4π²ε₀²)² · |α|² / r² · (1 + cos²θ) / 2</span>
        <small>其中 k = 2π/λ，由此得到特征性的 λ⁻⁴ 波长依赖性。</small>
      </div>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>截面标度关系。</strong>由于 σ ∝ λ⁻⁴，400 nm 与 700 nm 处的散射比约为 (700/400)⁴ ≈ 8 倍。与太阳光谱辐射亮度及明视觉敏感曲线 <i>V</i>(λ) 相叠加，使散射天空光在感知上以蓝色为主。</li>
        <li><strong>各向异性与去极化。</strong>空气分子（N₂、O₂）的极化率张量具有各向异性，线偏振光散射后部分去极化；空气的去极化度约为 1.4%。天空的偏振（在距太阳 90° 方向上最强）正是偶极子辐射图案的直接结果。</li>
        <li><strong>光学厚度与角度依赖。</strong>观测到的辐射亮度取决于视线方向上的光学厚度 τ(λ)；太阳低垂时更长的斜程路径（sec θ₀ 近似，球面大气需更严格的处理）解释了太阳的变红，以及 τ(λ_blue) ≫ 1 时红/橙色调的增强。</li>
        <li><strong>适用区域的边界。</strong>当 2πa/λ ~ 1 时，瑞利近似失效；对云中的水滴（a ~ 1–100 μm ≫ λ），适用<em>米氏散射</em>，它几乎与波长无关，因此云呈白色。瑞利散射同样解释了人眼玻璃体的蓝色以及气态巨行星天空的蓝色（甲烷的吸收会改变海王星/天王星上的图景）。</li>
        <li><strong>分子形状因子。</strong>严格来说，α 是频率的函数（洛伦兹振子色散），但在 N₂ 和 O₂ 的可见光波段，其变化相对于显式的 k⁴ 因子较弱，因此 λ⁻⁴ 定律在百分之几的精度内成立。从量子力学角度看，由极化率二阶微扰理论可得到同样的结果；拉曼线（Bjerknes/Raman）作为小的结构化分量出现于弹性瑞利背景之外。</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>简而言之：</strong>天空是蓝色的，因为小分子的弹性散射带有 λ⁻⁴ 权重，与太阳光谱和人眼光谱响应相卷积后，在感知上峰值落在蓝色。</p>
    </div>
  </div>

